98193359375, -0.00212860107421875, -0.0183258056640625, 0.001316070556640625, -0.0328369140625, -0.00244903564453125, 0.0501708984375, 0.0017652511596679688, -0.00888824462890625, 0.0149078369140625, 0.0272216796875, 0.01436614990234375, 0.0113372802734375, -0.0268402099609375, -0.03448486328125, 0.0184173583984375, 0.01085662841796875, 0.01108551025390625, 0.00630950927734375, -0.004077911376953125, -0.0024318695068359375, 0.00679779052734375, -0.010528564453125, 0.018402099609375, 0.01788330078125, 0.0035572052001953125, -0.0140380859375, 0.0031757354736328125, -0.0223541259765625, -0.0013179779052734375, -0.0009293556213378906, 0.0130157470703125, 0.041259765625, 0.0243682861328125, -0.0167388916015625, 0.0025577545166015625, 0.032501220703125, -0.00044655799865722656, 0.064697265625, -0.050018310546875, 0.032745361328125, 0.0030155181884765625, -0.034393310546875, -0.0006866455078125, -0.027740478515625, 0.006412506103515625, 0.012908935546875, -0.039703369140625, -0.00018036365509033203, -0.007030487060546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supply. However, they must address the challenges posed by increasingly heterogeneous and partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and complex meshed topologies. This paper investigates the global optimization of district heating network operations over multiple time steps, using a stationary yet realistic nonlinear network model. To enhance the performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for single time-step optimization, several novel methodological improvements are introduced: exclusion of cyclic flows, exploitation of symmetry between supply and return networks, reduction of temperature mixing constraints, and the development of new primal heuristics and branching rules. These methods are evaluated on both synthetic and real-world benchmark network instances featuring cycles and multiple suppliers. On the synthetic benchmarks, the proposed techniques more than double the number of solved instances and reduce runtime by over half. For the real-world benchmarks, the algorithm delivers solutions with guaranteed quality within reasonable computation times. For multi-step problems coupled by thermal storage, a time decomposition approach is explored. Under practically reasonable assumptions, this approach is proven to yield optimal solutions. In a small example network, the decomposition computes optimal solutions in under a second, whereas solving the full time-coupled problem directly is infeasible within an hour."}}